import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.1C62pOns.js";const r="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/grupos-detalhe.png",s="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/usuarios-detalhe.png",i="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/configuracoes.png",d="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/consumo-tokens.png",h="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/import-export.png",w=JSON.parse('{"title":"Administration","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/interface/administracao.md","filePath":"en/interface/administracao.md","lastUpdated":1787856241000}'),l={name:"en/interface/administracao.md"};function p(c,e,u,g,m,f){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="administration" tabindex="-1">Administration <a class="header-anchor" href="#administration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Administration&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Everything on this page is visible to <strong>master</strong> users only.</p><h2 id="groups" tabindex="-1">Groups <a class="header-anchor" href="#groups" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Groups&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+r+'" alt="grupos-detalhe"></p><p>A group is the platform&#39;s unit of access. It gathers users on one side and releases resources on the other: agents, teams, workflows, planners, MCP services and Apps.</p><p>Each release has a <strong>mode</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Read</strong> — open and run.</li><li><strong>Write</strong> — open, run and save.</li></ul><p>A user can be in several groups; they see the union of what their groups released, with the most permissive mode among them.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">A user with no group sees nothing</p><p>And that is the correct behaviour, not a defect. Whoever signs up through the create-account screen arrives with no group at all, and depends on a master to start using anything.</p></div><h2 id="users" tabindex="-1">Users <a class="header-anchor" href="#users" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Users&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+s+'" alt="usuarios-detalhe"></p><p>Name, e-mail, phone, password and two switches that decide a great deal:</p><p><strong>Master</strong> — sees everything and administers everything, with no group involved. Master is not &quot;power user&quot;: it is whoever can read an API key, change settings and export the database. Hand it out sparingly.</p><p><strong>Disabled</strong> — the user can no longer sign in, but still exists, and with them the history of runs and conversations. That is what you use when someone leaves the company; deleting would erase the trail.</p><h2 id="settings" tabindex="-1">Settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#settings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Settings {#settings}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+i+'" alt="configuracoes"></p><h3 id="e-mail" tabindex="-1">E-mail <a class="header-anchor" href="#e-mail" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;E-mail&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The SMTP the platform uses for &quot;forgot my password&quot; and for the usage alerts. Until it is configured, neither of those goes out at all.</p><h3 id="licence" tabindex="-1">Licence <a class="header-anchor" href="#licence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Licence&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Serial and control number of the installation.</p><h3 id="environment-variables" tabindex="-1">Environment variables <a class="header-anchor" href="#environment-variables" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Environment variables&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Name/value pairs available to the code of workflows, planners, MCP tools and App backends. This is where keys and addresses live that you do not want written in the middle of the code — and that differ from one environment to another.</p><h3 id="security" tabindex="-1">Security <a class="header-anchor" href="#security" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Security&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Workflow API token</strong> — the secret that authenticates whoever triggers a workflow from outside the platform.</p><p><strong>Pegasus</strong> — the built-in co-pilot. On, it appears in the code and data-source screens, suggesting Python, flows and CSS selectors. It requires a chosen model; without one, the platform refuses to save it enabled.</p><h3 id="retention" tabindex="-1">Retention <a class="header-anchor" href="#retention" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Retention&quot;">​</a></h3><p>How much run history to keep. Three limits, applied by a periodic cleanup:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Limit</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Execution days</td><td>30</td><td>Removes runs older than that.</td></tr><tr><td>Log days</td><td>7</td><td>Removes the detailed log, <strong>keeping the run header</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>Executions per workflow</td><td>200</td><td>Trims the excess of a workflow that runs a lot.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The first two go well together: you still see that a run from 20 days ago happened and how long it took, without carrying its log line by line.</p><p>Runs <strong>still in progress are never pruned</strong>, however old they are.</p><p>The default log level for new workflows also lives here.</p><h2 id="token-usage" tabindex="-1">Token usage <a class="header-anchor" href="#token-usage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Token usage {#token-usage}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+d+'" alt="consumo-tokens"></p><p>How much each model consumed, in tokens and in cost, with a daily and a monthly report.</p><p>Cost depends on the price on the model&#39;s record. With no price, you get a token count and no figure — which is already enough to find the agent that is spending too much.</p><p>The alert and the blocking cap are configured <strong>on the model&#39;s record</strong>, not here. See <a href="/pro-agent-hub-installer/en/interface/provedores-modelos#price-and-limits">Providers and models</a>.</p><h2 id="import-and-export" tabindex="-1">Import and export <a class="header-anchor" href="#import-and-export" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Import and export {#import-and-export}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+h+'" alt="import-export"></p><p>Copies records between environments — from development to production, or from one installation to another.</p><p>The selection is a tree: pick whole groups (Workflows, Agents, Providers…) or specific records inside each one.</p><h3 id="dependencies-come-along" tabindex="-1">Dependencies come along <a class="header-anchor" href="#dependencies-come-along" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dependencies come along&quot;">​</a></h3><p>An agent without its model does not work. So, on export (and on import), <strong>the records your selection depends on are included even if you do not tick them</strong> — unless you turn the option off. Ticking a workflow brings the agent it calls, that agent&#39;s model and that model&#39;s provider.</p><p>Before you confirm, the screen shows what was added by dependency.</p><h3 id="on-import" tabindex="-1">On import <a class="header-anchor" href="#on-import" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;On import&quot;">​</a></h3><p>What already exists at the destination is <strong>recognised by description</strong>, not by ID. An existing record is kept as it is, and the links point at it — the IDs from the file are remapped to the destination&#39;s.</p><p>That means re-importing does not undo a change made by hand at the destination. It is deliberate: importing is for bringing over what is missing, not for overwriting the environment.</p><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Groups and users deserve attention</p><p>Bringing groups and users from another environment rewrites who has access to what. The screen flags those groups and asks for confirmation — read the summary before accepting.</p></div><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Secrets go out encrypted — with a key that lives in the source</p><p>Provider API keys, database connection passwords, the SMTP password, webhook tokens and the environment variables all leave encrypted in the file. But the default key is in the source code: that protects against casual reading by whoever opens the JSON, <strong>not</strong> against someone determined.</p><p>For the file to be genuinely confidential, set the <code>PRO_AI_EXPORT_SECRET</code> environment variable on both installations. With it, the file only opens where the same value is configured — and a file with the wrong key is refused with a clear message, instead of writing rubbish where the password should be.</p><p>Either way: the export file is sensitive material. Store it as such and delete it once you have imported.</p></div>',48)])])}const v=t(l,[["render",p]]);export{w as __pageData,v as default};
